Page description: Cyberpunk maker lab in rural Asturias. Experimenting with AI, robotics, TinyGo, computer vision, and open-source hardware. Where code meets countryside. StudioHackspace[ SYSTEM ACTIVE ]HACKSPACEHACKSPACEOPEN SOURCE × AI × ROBOTICS × INNOVATIONTinyGoGoLangGobotGoCVComputer VisionAIR&D[ PROTOCOL ]OPEN SOURCEMANIFESTO[01]HACK_EVERYTHINGBreak systems apart. Understand how they work. Rebuild them better. Open source means transparent, auditable, improvable code.[02]BUILD_FUTUREFrom TinyGo microcontrollers to computer vision systems, we prototype the technology that doesn''t exist yet.[03]SHARE_KNOWLEDGEEvery project documented. Every failure a lesson. Every breakthrough shared with the global maker community.""The question isn''t whether machines think — it''s whether we still do.— RON EVANS[ VISIONARY THINKING ]Ron Evans'' 2019 VisionRon Evans'' groundbreaking vision from LA PIPA Hackspace in 2019 about data, machine learning, and the real world. Six years later in 2025, his predictions have proven remarkably prescient. Working alongside Alex Lawton, they saw the future clearly—and that future is now our present reality. What a vision.[ FEATURED INTERVIEW ]Channel 9 Microsoft TVRon Evans × Alex Lawton // hosted by Aaron Wislang[ COMMUNITY ]Go Developers Network (GDN)Ron Evans at LA PIPA Hackspace program with The Go Developers Network, bringing together people who code in Go from meetup groups organized all over the planet.[ TECHNOLOGY STACK ]THE_LABGoLang_Ecosystem→TinyGoMicrocontroller programming→GobotRobotics & IoT framework→GoCVComputer vision in GoAI_Systems→Machine LearningNeural networks & models→Computer VisionImage processing & analysis→Edge AIOn-device intelligenceRobotics_Lab→DronesAutonomous flight systems→Embedded SystemsMicrocontroller projects→Sensors & ActuatorsPhysical computingR&D_Projects→ExperimentationRapid prototyping→InnovationPushing boundaries→Open SourceCommunity-driven developmentGO TIME LIVE! #gotimefmRON EVANSLOOKING BACKAT GO FROM2053How did Go survive the thirty years between today and the future perspective shared by Ron Evans (deadprogram), Mat Ryer, and Natalie Pistunovich? What changed about the language to keep it relevant for all these years?ABOUT THE EPISODEStreamed live on 14 June 2022, this special Go Time episode features LA PIPA Hackspace community member Ron Evans (deadprogram) alongside Mat Ryer and Natalie Pistunovich in a fascinating thought experiment about the future of the Go programming language.The conversation explores how Go evolved over thirty years, what changes kept it relevant, and the innovations that shaped programming from a 2053 perspective.Chat with the community in the #gotimefm channel of Gophers SlackMORE FROM RON EVANSRon also appeared on the Changelog podcast discussing open source hardware, robotics, and the maker movement that drives communities like LA PIPA Hackspace.WATCH THE FULL EPISODEFEATURING• Ron Evans (deadprogram)• Mat Ryer• Natalie Pistunovich⚡PROTOTYPEITERATESHAREWe don''t believe in keeping knowledge locked up. Everything we learn, every failure, every breakthrough—we document and share with the community.Open source isn''t just a licensing model.',848,'46d99b66bcfcecc30b9adc42e87d619ce1c55b298e905198502dc4ffe8409671','en','captured_public_claim','public',true,'{"chunk_profile":"LP-RAG-CHUNK-1.0","max_characters":3600,"overlap_characters":400,"token_count_method":"ceil_utf16_characters_divided_by_4"}'::jsonb) on conflict (chunk_id) do update set content=excluded.content,token_count=excluded.token_count,content_sha256=excluded.content_sha256,verification_status=excluded.verification_status,access_scope=excluded.access_scope,active=true,metadata=excluded.metadata,updated_at=now();
